# The audio itself. Hundreds of gigabytes of generated speech do not belong in
# git; the tools rebuild it from the corpus and it ships as release assets.
/sounds/
# The work list, rebuilt from the corpus in seconds and far too big to keep.
/Data/lines.jsonl
/build/
/.venv/
# The stand-in clips are the exception to "audio does not belong in git". They
# are 54 KB, the engine names them at a fixed path, and CurseForge builds from a
# tagged commit -- so ignoring them meant the "play a placeholder" setting was
# silently silent for every released user while working perfectly on the machine
# that built it. Working files in demo/ are still ignored; these two are not.
/demo/*
!/demo/male.ogg
!/demo/female.ogg
generate*.log
generate.err
# The Voxtral run's own working files: its log, the heartbeat the watchdog
# reads, and the watchdog's own log. All three are rewritten every run.
voxtral*.log
voxtral.progress
# Cross-check fixtures, regenerated by the tools that write them.
tests/_texts.txt
tests/_counts.txt
# A megabyte each, and grouping.test.lua tells you which tool to run when they
# are missing rather than failing at you. tests/spans.test.lua needs none of
# them, so a bare checkout still has one test of the sentence grouping.
tests/_grouping_texts.txt
tests/_grouping_spans.txt
tests/_grouping_fields.txt
tests/_grouping_lengths.txt
tests/_grouping_starts.txt
/.scratch/
# Everything under voices/ belongs to the abandoned approach. The plan was to
# clone a voice per race from reference recordings; the reader that was chosen
# has its audio encoder removed from the open weights and cannot clone at all,
# so nothing here is read by anything that still runs.
#
# The two exceptions this list used to carry -- narrator.wav and female.wav,
# kept so somebody else could reproduce the voice -- defended files that are now
# deleted, and the voice they described is not the voice that ships. Ignored
# whole rather than deleted: the 63 mp3 in here are game recordings that must
# never be published, and an ignore rule is the thing that guarantees it.
/voices/
# The tools of that same approach. They import Chatterbox, they read voices/,
# and one of them opens a file that no longer exists. Kept on disk because they
# are how the four readers were measured against each other, which is the one
# claim in the README that cannot be re-derived cheaply -- but they are not
# source for anything that is built today.
/Tools/bakeoff.py
/Tools/batch_speed.py
/Tools/check_faithful.py
/Tools/compare_models.py
/Tools/import_voices.py
/Tools/sample.py
/Tools/trim_references.py
/Tools/tune.py
# generate.py and demo.py are not in that list because they are already tracked,
# and a rule that cannot take effect is a lie about what the repository holds.
# They stay as history: generate.py is the Chatterbox generator every earlier
# measurement in the README was made with.
/release/
/curseforge/
*.zip
__pycache__/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*scratchpad*
*AppData*
# What the recogniser heard, rebuilt by Tools/listen_words.py in seven hours.
# Evidence rather than source, and 104,274 lines of it.
/heard.jsonl
# The repair run's own working files, on the same footing as heard.jsonl above:
# what was flagged, what each pass decided, and what the disk said afterwards.
# Every one is rebuilt by the tool that writes it, and together they are the
# evidence for one run rather than anything the addon is built from.
#
# The family, not a list of names. Five of these were written out one at a time
# and the sixth pass invented four more -- jfix.jsonl, jfix2.jsonl and two
# reports -- which arrived untracked and unignored, exactly as the logs below
# did before they were given one pattern. No .jsonl in this repository has ever
# been source: the work list is /Data/lines.jsonl above, and everything else at
# the root is something a tool wrote down about a run.
/*.jsonl
# Written by Tools/verify_repairs.py and its per-run variants, which land beside
# the reports rather than at the root when a run names its own output.
/verified*.jsonl
# A run's hand-offs between its own passes -- the path list one tool writes for
# the next to read. Root only, so Tools/requirements.txt, the one .txt in this
# repository that is source, is untouched.
/*.txt
# Every log a repair run drops at the root. Named one at a time until three
# separate patterns had each missed the next one written -- round2.log, then
# fillerfix.log -- so the rule is the class now: nothing this addon ships is a
# .log, and the ones the tools write are rebuilt by the tool that writes them.
/*.log
